From 928763aded0d968938cada8983dc01ffd234eddd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 10:38:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] video: a VD_YCK yield site is a 3-byte patched call (SPEC.md
 98.3.18.11)

LOADALL-PLAN 9.2's option 1. A yield site was twelve bytes: a jump over
itself (EB 0A) and an inline `cmp si, [cs:vd_ylim] / jb / call vd_yield`,
with an in-place session patching the jump to two NOPs. Now a site is
three bytes, `test ax, rel16` (A9), whose immediate is already a near
call's displacement to vd_yield. vd_ypatch flips the one byte to E8, which
makes the site `call vd_yield`, and vd_yield makes the compare itself
(`cmp / jae .go / ret`). Flags are dead at every site.

The C160 decoder's eight sites go too (VD_NOYCK). They were never in
vd_ytab and so never patched, and C160 is decoded only through the
shadow, which neither vp_xipq nor vp_xbipq takes.

Bytes: -258 in every arm. Default 53,646 -> 53,388, VP_NOLIVESND
53,417 -> 53,159, VPDIAG 55,006 -> 54,748, bss 4,144 unchanged.

8088, measured on MartyPC's 5150 (VIDBENCH, synthetic XDC frames): every
frame is faster, to the screen and to RAM. An empty frame is -54 cycles
(8 sites, ~6.7 a site) and the others -35 to -90. The vidfskeys --cost
hook means with the text down: herc -69, cga4 -46, vga8 -68,
vga4m3 -810, modexflip -115. With the text up they are within noise.

The site-visit counts are counted exactly on the host from the lists, and
cross-checked on MartyPC: an armed vidbench (every site `call` to a `ret`)
costs 40 +-1 cycles a visit on every row at the host's counts (8, 9 and
10 a frame).

286 in place: a call and a ret where there were two NOPs and a taken jb.
That is +13 clocks a site visit, PREDICTED from Intel's table. It comes to
9-38 visits a frame on the demo files (82 the most), so 7-31 us at
16 MHz, 0.25-0.6% of the Victory's measured decode on a real synthetic
frame (an empty frame +6.5 us). A 286 play that copies gets back ~9 clocks
a site against the jump the Victory measured at ~12.

Parity is kept: VD_GATE pins its own .seg in the dead byte after its jump
on, VD_NSPAN pins its round's head, and pads restore the long spans'. No
identical routine moved by an odd amount in any arm. The only flips are
jump targets going odd -> even.

tests/vidxip.py reads the first site's byte (A9 after a session). Broken
on purpose, both ways: without the E8 store, 0 yields; without the A9
restore, "left live (E8)".

